H-SYNC: A Formal Protocol for Institutional Synchronization, Verification, and Accountability under Conditions of Technological Asynchrony

A Theoretical Contribution

Working Paper — H-SYNC v0.3

Ouadi Maakoul and AI TEAM 

Abstract

We present H-SYNC (Human Institutional Synchronization, Verification & Accountability Protocol), a formal protocol for inter-institutional coordination under conditions in which the characteristic time of technological change is smaller than the characteristic time of institutional adaptation. The protocol does not require the creation of a sovereign authority. We derive its axioms from documented failure modes in the U.S. Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), as audited by the Government Accountability Office (GAO) in GAO-26-108615, GAO-26-108192, and GAO-26-109096. We formalize institutional asynchrony via a dimensionless synchronization gap, distinguish four epistemic states of a quantitative claim (claimed, estimated, verified, realized), and propose a verification architecture grounded in provenance, protected uncertainty, distributed verification, and a graded cooperation obligation. We specify a three-dimensional evaluation framework (VerifiedResolution, JustifiedAbstention, FalseCertaintyRate) grounded in selective prediction theory rather than probabilistic forecast scoring, stratified by claim stakes, and provide a traceability matrix mapping each GAO finding to an axiom and a falsification criterion. This is a purely theoretical contribution. No experiments have been executed, no code has been trained or run, and no physical or empirical tests have been performed. The calibration procedures in §10 are falsification criteria proposed for future work, not results. The protocol is specified, not validated.

Keywords: institutional asynchrony, provenance, verification, uncertainty, AI governance, protocol design, falsifiability, selective prediction.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The diffusion of artificial intelligence across social domains increasingly exceeds the rate at which human institutions coordinate their representations of the world. Kim (2025) models this asynchrony using a logistic growth framework and reports that advancing the start of intervention has roughly twice the relative effectiveness of accelerating enforcement, though we note that this specific quantitative result has not been independently verified by the present authors and is cited as reported. The asymmetry is structural rather than contingent: institutional adaptation is bounded by procedural, legal, and cognitive constraints that do not scale with computational capability.

This paper addresses a narrow question: what is the minimal formal protocol by which heterogeneous institutions can coordinate verification without surrendering authority to a central sovereign? We do not propose a new institution. We propose an interface specification.

1.2 The Empirical Anchor

The U.S. Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), established by Executive Order 14158 in January 2025, provides a documented case of verification failure. Three GAO audits form the empirical basis:

GAO-26-108615 (August 2026) reports that, as of July 7, 2026, DOGE claimed **$110.3 billion** in savings across contracts, grants, and leases. GAO found that $27.4 billion of claimed contract savings derived from 2,503 contracts on which no termination action was taken; approximately $34.6 billion of the $61.02 billion contract figure derived from contracts never terminated or unverifiable as terminated; 96% of reported grant savings (approximately $47.2 billion) lacked a verifiable method; 108 of 264 lease terminations were already in process before DOGE was established; and a $1.7 billion Defense Health Agency claim produced no measurable action or savings.

GAO-26-108192 (September 2026) documents a second failure mode: the inability to verify whether embedded actors who accessed sensitive systems did so under auditable controls. GAO found that four agencies reported DOGE teams accessing more than 23 systems collectively, that permissions could not be determined, and that two agencies (SBA and VA) did not respond to requests, while SEC and NOAA challenged GAO's authority.

GAO-26-109096 (September 3, 2026) documents a third failure mode: stale claims. DHS reported that contract terminations from January 2025 through September 2025 could potentially avoid more than $10.5 billion in costs. GAO found that DHS deobligated only **$92 million** across 438 terminated contracts, and that the department's methodology relied on the maximum amount that could have been spent under the canceled contracts rather than actual deobligations . GAO further found that DHS's reported cost avoidance did not account for re-obligated funds, including approximately $1.7 billion re-obligated elsewhere . This case is particularly relevant because the claim was not fraudulent at the moment of issuance — it was a projection — but it became stale, and H-SYNC's temporality axiom (H2) is designed to detect exactly this failure.

These are not allegations of fraud. They are documented failures of verification infrastructure.

1.3 Scope and Epistemic Status

This paper is purely theoretical. We make no claim to have executed any test, trained any model, or conducted any experiment. We do not report empirical results. The GAO reports are used as documented failure modes — sources of axioms, not data against which our protocol has been validated. The calibration procedures in §10 are stated as falsification criteria: conditions under which the protocol, if implemented, would be shown to fail. Their execution is future work.

This epistemic modesty is deliberate. A protocol that claims validation without execution is a liability. A protocol that specifies its own falsification criteria is an invitation to criticism, which is the condition of academic credibility.

1.4 Contributions

1. A formal definition of institutional asynchrony using dimensionless time constants, with all values labeled as illustrative pending measurement (§4).
2. An axiomatic specification of a minimal verification protocol, H1–H11, with an explicit derivation matrix (§6, §10.3).
3. A graded cooperation obligation that distinguishes justified from unjustified refusal (§8).
4. A three-dimensional evaluation framework grounded in selective prediction theory, not probabilistic forecast scoring (§9).
5. An extension of the institutional interface to include a system-access ledger (§6.5).
6. A specification of Test 4 (adversarial polarity) as a falsification criterion (§10.2).

1.5 Organization

§2 reviews related work. §3 establishes notation. §4 formalizes asynchrony. §5 formalizes the verification problem. §6 specifies the protocol axiomatically. §7 derives theoretical properties with corrected proofs. §8 formalizes the graded cooperation obligation. §9 develops evaluation theory. §10 specifies calibration and falsification criteria. §11 discusses limitations. §12 concludes.

