2. Related Work

2.1 Socio-Institutional Asynchrony

Kim (2025) formalizes the gap between technological and institutional rates using coupled logistic growth. The model assumes a compute doubling time of approximately six months and reports that intervention timing dominates enforcement efficiency. Our work adopts the logistic framing but replaces the original gap expression with a dimensionless ratio of characteristic times. We do not independently verify Kim's specific quantitative claims.

2.2 Provenance and Verification

The W3C PROV data model (2013) specifies a generic representation of entities, activities, and agents. PROV-AGENT extends this for agentic workflows. We adopt PROV as the schema for H1 and do not propose a new provenance ontology.

2.3 Selective Prediction and Abstention

The selective prediction literature formalizes the tradeoff between coverage (the fraction of inputs on which a system makes a prediction) and risk (the error rate on accepted inputs) . A system that abstains on uncertain cases sacrifices coverage in favor of reduced error. This framework is more appropriate for H-SYNC's evaluation than probabilistic forecast scoring (Brier, Murphy), because H-SYNC's states are categorical and abstention is a first-class outcome.

2.4 Audit Practice and Scope Limitations

Audit practice has long recognized the disclaimer of opinion when evidence is withheld — a scope limitation. Subjective logic and three-valued logic already give "unknown" a formal place. H-SYNC's protected uncertainty state U inherits from these traditions; its novelty is not the concept of unknown but the elevation of unknown to an auditable registry state with a cooperation obligation attached.

2.5 AI Governance Frameworks

The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (2023) advises recording uncertainty, gaps, and not-applicable rationale. The OECD's 2026 input emphasizes interoperability and traceability. H-SYNC operates at a different level: not a risk-management framework for a single system, but a coordination protocol between institutions.

2.6 Gap

No existing framework specifies (a) a first-class unverifiable state with a registry event, (b) a graded cooperation obligation distinguishing justified from unjustified refusal, or (c) a system-access ledger at the institutional interface.

