4. The Synchronization Gap

4.1 Definition

Definition 7 (Synchronization Gap).

\Gamma(t) = \log\left( \frac{\tau_S(t)}{\tau_A(t)} \right) \tag{1}

The choice of logarithm renders \Gamma dimensionless. When \Gamma > 0, institutional coordination is slower than technological change.

Proposition 1 (Sign Interpretation). \Gamma > 0 \iff \tau_S > \tau_A.

Proof. Immediate from monotonicity of \log. \square

Remark 2. \Gamma and \Sigma do not appear in any axiom, state transition, or evaluation metric of the protocol. They are framing devices, not load-bearing components. We state this explicitly to avoid the v0.2 error of presenting decorative formalism as if it were operational.

4.2 Synchronization Capacity

Definition 8 (Synchronization Capacity).

\Sigma(t) = \left( C^{w_C} D^{w_D} I^{w_I} R^{w_R} V^{w_V} \right)^{1 / \sum_j w_j} \tag{2}

where C, D, I, R, V \in [0,1] denote compatibility, inverse delay, interoperability, response capacity, and verification capacity. The geometric mean penalizes any single component approaching zero.

Definition 9 (Desynchronization Zone). A desynchronization zone exists when \Gamma(t) > 0 \land \Sigma(t) < \Sigma_{\min} for a policy threshold \Sigma_{\min} \in (0,1).

Remark 3. \Sigma_{\min} is a policy parameter, not a derived constant.

