5. The Verification Problem

5.1 Four Typed States of a Claim

Definition 10 (Claim). A claim c is a tuple c = (a, q, t, \pi) where a is an assertion, q a quantity, t a timestamp, and \pi a producer.

Definition 11 (Four States). For a claim c:

· S_{\text{claimed}}(c): asserted value;
· S_{\text{estimated}}(c): value produced by a method;
· S_{\text{verified}}(c): value independently confirmed;
· S_{\text{realized}}(c): value measured after implementation.

Axiom 1 (Typed States). The four states are distinct types. They may coincide in value, but they are not interchangeable. Formally, there exist projection functions \pi_{\text{cl}}, \pi_{\text{est}}, \pi_{\text{ver}}, \pi_{\text{real}}: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R} \cup \{\bot\} such that the assertion of equality S_{\text{claimed}} = S_{\text{realized}} is itself a claim requiring verification.

Remark 4. The v0.2 manuscript stated this as a chain of inequalities S_{\text{claimed}} \neq S_{\text{estimated}} \neq S_{\text{verified}} \neq S_{\text{realized}}. That formulation is not a valid axiom: it asserts non-identity as a universal, whereas the correct formulation is that the four are distinct types whose coincidence is contingent and must be demonstrated. The DOGE case illustrates the gap: the claimed figure ($110.3B) differed from the estimated figure (by unverifiable method), which differed from the verified figure (approximately 4% with support, and that 4% figure applies to grant savings only), which differed from the realized figure (the DHA $1.7B is a single contract, not the total realized effect). We do not claim the four states are universally unequal. We claim that their identification requires evidence.

Axiom 2 (No-Verification). \neg V(c) \Rightarrow \neg \text{Final}(c) where \text{Final}(c) denotes a state in \{C, R\}.

5.2 The Protected Uncertainty State

Definition 12 (Unverifiable). A claim c is unverifiable with available data iff no sequence of admissible evidence operations reconstructs S_{\text{verified}}(c).

Axiom 3 (U ≠ 0). The unverifiable state U is a first-class outcome distinct from the null or zero value.

