6. The H-SYNC Protocol: Axiomatic Specification

6.1 Invariants

H1 (Provenance). Every quantitative claim must be reconstructible from sources: E = (S, M, U, T, A, P, V) where S = source, M = method, U = uncertainty, T = timestamp, A = responsible actor, P = full provenance, V = version. This maps onto the W3C PROV Entity–Activity–Agent model.

Proposition 2 (Provenance ≠ Validity). \text{Prov}(c) \nRightarrow \text{Valid}(c).

Proof. A claim may be perfectly reconstructible from sources and still be false. \square

H2 (Temporality). Every projection has a validity period t_{\text{issue}} \to t_{\text{review}} \to t_{\text{expiry}}. After expiry without new evidence, S_p \to U.

H3 (Attribution). H-SYNC records \mathcal{C} = (I, B, CF, P, H) where I = intervention, B = baseline, CF = counterfactual, P = comparison population, H = identification assumptions.

H4 (Protected Uncertainty). U is a valid outcome. Pressure to suppress or artificially convert a U state is itself an auditable event.

H5 (Distributed Verification). Producer \neq Sole Verifier.

H6 (Versioning). Every result carries (V_P, V_M, V_D) for protocol, method, and data versions.

H7 (Contestability). Any legitimately affected party can file a challenge: Claim \to Challenge \to Review \to Resolution.

H8 (Failure Transparency). Missing data, contradictions, non-cooperation, methodological limits, and known errors are recorded.

H9 (Anti-Capture, Sketch). Auditor selection is by sortition from a pre-registered pool. Pool membership is versioned (V_P). No verifier serves more than N consecutive cases for the same producer. Critical claims require \geq 2 independent verifiers.

Remark 5. H9 is a design sketch, not a fully specified mechanism. It does not specify who maintains the pool, who versions V_P, or who enforces the redundancy threshold. These are open problems. We state them as such rather than claiming a solution we do not have.

H10 (Reversibility). C(t_1) \to C'(t_2) without this revision being treated as institutional failure.

H11 (Cooperation Obligation, Graded). A registry event is triggered when a producer refuses verification access. The event records a graded cooperation level and a justification flag. If cooperation is refused and no independent verification is possible, the claim state becomes U. See §8 for the full specification.

6.2 State Space

Definition 13 (Registry State Space).

Z = \{N, A, S, V, C, R, U, D\} \tag{3}

State Meaning Final?
N No anomaly established No (see T1′)
A Anomaly detected No
S Suspicion requiring examination No
V Verification in progress No
C Confirmed by competent authority Yes
R Result/recovery actually measured Yes
U Unverifiable with available data Yes
D Disputed (challenge filed) No

6.3 Transition Function

Definition 14 (Transition Function). Let E_t denote the evidence state at time t. A transition function is \delta: Z \times E_t \to Z subject to:

· (T1′) \delta(N, \emptyset) = U. Absence of evidence does not yield "no anomaly." This corrects the v0.2 formulation \delta(N, \emptyset) = N, which created a suppression channel: a producer could withhold evidence and obtain a favorable state.
· (T2) \delta(z, \emptyset) = U for z \in \{A, S, V, C, R\}.
· (T3) \delta(C, \text{challenge}) = D.
· (T4) \delta(U, \text{cooperation}) = V.
· (T5) \delta(z, \text{non-cooperation}) = U for any z requiring verification.
· (T6) \delta(U, \text{new evidence}) = V.

Proposition 3 (Absorbing U under Non-Cooperation). If non-cooperation persists indefinitely, the state converges to U.

Proof. By repeated application of (T5). \square

6.4 Public vs. Auditor Verifiability

Definition 15 (Verifiability Classes). A claim is publicly verifiable if its provenance chain P is reconstructible using only public records. A claim is auditor-verifiable if P is reconstructible by an authorized auditor under confidentiality constraints.

Remark 6. The v0.2 manuscript conflated these, making every legitimately non-public claim U. This was too strong. The corrected formulation adds a state distinction: a claim that is auditor-verifiable but not publicly verifiable may be V in the registry with an access-controlled provenance chain, provided the auditor's authorization chain X is recorded.

6.5 Institutional Interface (Extended)

Definition 16 (Interface).

I = (O, R, C, A, T, E, P, V, S, X) \tag{4}

where the first eight components are as in v0.1, and S = system-access ledger (who accessed what system, under what authority, with what controls), X = authorization chain (delegation, scope, duration).

Principle 1 (Minimize Centralized Authority — Design Goal). H-SYNC is designed to minimize the authority required for coordination. It does not eliminate all centralized authority: the registry requires governance, and the sortition pool in H9 requires a maintainer. We state this as a design goal, not an achieved property. The governance of the registry itself is an open problem.

Remark 7. The v0.2 manuscript stated H-SYNC ≠ New Authority as an assertion. A critic correctly noted that the sortition pool must be maintained by someone, making the registry a sovereign in miniature. We accept this criticism and downgrade the claim.

6.6 Minimum Viable Adoption Hook

Any claim published by a signatory institution without a reconstructible provenance chain is automatically state U in the registry. The registry's U-list is published.

