7. Theoretical Properties (Corrected)

7.1 Soundness of Abstention

Definition 17 (Final State). A state is final if it is in \{C, R, U\}. Non-final states are \{N, A, S, V, D\}.

Proposition 4 (Sound Abstention — Corrected). If evidence is insufficient for a final claim, H-SYNC returns a state in \{U\} rather than a default in \{C, R\}.

Proof. By Axiom 2, \neg V(c) \Rightarrow \neg \text{Final}(c) where Final = \{C, R\}. By (T1′) and (T2), the only state reachable from \{N, A, S, V, C, R\} under \emptyset evidence is U. Note that \{A, S, V\} are non-final but are not U; they represent in-progress states, not resolved claims. The proposition concerns resolved claims. \square

Remark 8. The v0.2 proof claimed "the only non-final state is U," which is false: N, A, S, V, D are non-final. The corrected proof defines "final" explicitly and restricts the claim to resolved states.

7.2 Reversibility

Proposition 5 (Non-Monotonicity). The map t \mapsto \text{State}(c, t) is not monotone in the sense that C(t_1) does not preclude C'(t_2) with C' \neq C. Proof. By H10. \square

7.3 Anti-Capture (Remark, Not Proposition)

Remark 9. Under H9, verifier selection is independent of producer identity modulo pool composition. This reduces capture probability relative to producer-selected verification, but does not bound it: if the pool itself is captured, the bound is vacuous. We state this as a remark, not a proposition. A full mechanism-design treatment is an open problem.

7.4 Traceability

Proposition 6 (Traceability — Corrected). If H1 and H6 hold, then every claim in the registry is either publicly verifiable, auditor-verifiable, or in state U.

Proof. By H1, a claim without reconstructible provenance fails the provenance check. By Definition 15, reconstructibility is relative to a verifiability class. By H6, methods and data are versioned, so reconstruction is well-defined within each class. By the adoption hook (§6.6), a claim failing both classes is U. \square

