Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 Notation and Conventions

We adopt the following conventions throughout.

Sets. \mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\}; \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} denotes non-negative reals; [0,1] the closed unit interval. For a set X, \mathcal{P}(X) denotes the power set and X^* the free monoid over X (finite sequences, including the empty sequence \epsilon).

Functions. For a function f: X \to Y, we write f[A] for the image of A \subseteq X, and f^{-1}[B] for the preimage of B \subseteq Y. The restriction of f to A \subseteq X is f|_A. The composition f \circ g means "apply g, then f."

Tuples. A k-tuple is written (x_1, \ldots, x_k), with projection \pi_i(x_1, \ldots, x_k) = x_i.

Time. Time is discrete: t \in \mathbb{N}. The convention t=0 denotes protocol initialization. Continuous time is avoided because the protocol's events are discrete (evidence arrival, challenge filing, cooperation refusal).

Partial orders. A partial order (P, \leq) satisfies reflexivity, antisymmetry, transitivity. The join (least upper bound) is \vee; the meet is \wedge. A lattice is a partial order in which every pair has a join and a meet. A complete lattice has joins and meets for every subset.

Many-valued logics. A valuation is a function v: \text{Prop} \to V for a set of propositions \text{Prop} and a value set V. Three-valued logic uses V = \{\mathsf{T}, \mathsf{F}, \mathsf{U}\}. Belnap's four-valued logic uses V = \{\mathsf{T}, \mathsf{F}, \mathsf{B}, \mathsf{N}\} for "true, false, both, neither." H-SYNC uses a three-valued fragment, with \mathsf{U} corresponding to "unverifiable."

A.2 Lattice Structure of the State Space

Definition A.1 (State Space). The registry state space is

Z = \{N, A, S, V, C, R, U, D\}

with the following intended interpretation.

Symbol Interpretation Epistemic Status
N No anomaly established Neutral / underdetermined
A Anomaly detected Epistemic positive
S Suspicion requiring examination Epistemic intermediate
V Verification in progress Procedural
C Confirmed by competent authority Epistemic positive (final)
R Result or recovery measured Epistemic positive (final)
U Unverifiable with available data Epistemic negative (final)
D Disputed Procedural

We impose a partial order \sqsubseteq on Z reflecting "resolution specificity." The order is motivated by the observation that U and C are both final but at opposite poles of information content, while N, A, S, V, D are intermediate.

Definition A.2 (Resolution Order). Define \sqsubseteq as the transitive closure of:

· N \sqsubseteq U;
· A \sqsubseteq V;
· S \sqsubseteq V;
· V \sqsubseteq C;
· V \sqsubseteq R;
· V \sqsubseteq U;
· D \sqsubseteq V.

Proposition A.1. (Z, \sqsubseteq) is a partial order but not a lattice.

Proof. Antisymmetry: no pair of distinct elements is mutually \sqsubseteq-related. Transitivity: by construction. Not a lattice: C and R have no upper bound in Z other than themselves, and neither dominates the other. Similarly U and C are incomparable. \square

Remark A.1. The non-lattice structure is a design choice, not an accident. It encodes the fact that confirmation (C) and measurement (R) are distinct epistemic achievements, neither of which subsumes the other.

Definition A.3 (Final and Non-Final States). Let F = \{C, R, U\} denote the set of final states. Let \bar{F} = Z \setminus F = \{N, A, S, V, D\} denote non-final states.

Definition A.4 (Absorbing Set). A set A \subseteq Z is absorbing under an input class \Sigma' if for every z \in A and s \in \Sigma', \delta(z,s) \in A.

Lemma A.1. F = \{C, R, U\} is absorbing under the empty-evidence input.

Proof. Transitions (T1′), (T2) of §6.3 map every state to U under \emptyset evidence. Once in U, (T1′) and (T2) do not apply; U transitions only under (T6) (new evidence) or (T4) (cooperation). Under \emptyset evidence, U is fixed. Hence F is absorbing under \emptyset. \square

A.3 Provenance Algebra

Definition A.5 (Provenance DAG). A provenance structure is a directed acyclic graph G_P = (V_P, E_P) where V_P is a set of nodes and E_P \subseteq V_P \times V_P is a set of directed edges satisfying acyclicity. Nodes are typed as entities, activities, or agents (following W3C PROV).

Definition A.6 (Claim Node). A claim c is represented as an entity node v_c \in V_P with an associated metadata tuple E = (S, M, U, T, A, P, V) where:

· S = source (a node reference),
· M = method (a reference to a versioned method specification),
· U = uncertainty (a real interval),
· T = timestamp,
· A = responsible actor (a node reference),
· P = provenance (the sub-DAG rooted at v_c),
· V = version tuple (V_P, V_M, V_D).

Definition A.7 (Reconstructibility). A claim c is reconstructible if there exists a finite traversal of G_P from v_c to a set of source nodes that are marked as trusted (either public or auditor-authorized). Formally, c is reconstructible iff v_c has a path in G_P to every source in S, and the traversal is finite.

Proposition A.2 (Acyclicity Guarantees Termination). If G_P is a DAG, then provenance reconstruction terminates.

Proof. Every traversal of a DAG terminates (finite nodes, no cycles). \square

Definition A.8 (Provenance Equivalence). Two claims c_1, c_2 are provenance-equivalent if their DAGs are isomorphic as typed graphs. Write c_1 \sim_P c_2.

Proposition A.3. \sim_P is an equivalence relation.

Proof. Reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity are inherited from graph isomorphism. \square

A.4 Three-Valued Logic and the U State

Definition A.9 (Three-Valued Logic). Let V_3 = \{\mathsf{T}, \mathsf{F}, \mathsf{U}\}. Define the operations:

· Negation: \neg \mathsf{T} = \mathsf{F}, \neg \mathsf{F} = \mathsf{T}, \neg \mathsf{U} = \mathsf{U}.
· Conjunction: \wedge is the minimum under the order \mathsf{F} < \mathsf{U} < \mathsf{T}, with \mathsf{F} \wedge x = \mathsf{F}, \mathsf{T} \wedge x = x, \mathsf{U} \wedge \mathsf{U} = \mathsf{U}.
· Disjunction: \vee is the maximum under the same order.

Proposition A.4. (V_3, \wedge, \vee) is a Kleene algebra.

Proof. Standard (Kleene 1952). De Morgan's laws hold, and the algebra is distributive. \square

Definition A.10 (Claim Evaluation). A claim c evaluates to a value in V_3: \mathsf{T} if verified true, \mathsf{F} if verified false, \mathsf{U} if unverifiable.

Proposition A.5 (U Is Neither True nor False). There is no valuation v on V_3 such that v(U) \in \{\mathsf{T}, \mathsf{F}\}.

Proof. By definition, U is a distinct third value. \square

Remark A.2. The elevation of U to a first-class value is the formal core of H4. The v0.2 manuscript used U as a state in the registry but did not commit to a specific logic. In v0.3 we adopt Kleene's three-valued logic as the valuation algebra; the choice is conservative and well-understood.

A.5 Selective Prediction Formalism

Definition A.11 (Selective Predictor). A selective predictor is a pair (f, g) where f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y} is a predictor and g: \mathcal{X} \to \{0,1\} is a selection function. A prediction is made when g(x) = 1; otherwise the predictor abstains.

Definition A.12 (Coverage). For a distribution \mathcal{D} over \mathcal{X}, coverage is

\text{cov}(f,g) = \Pr_{x \sim \mathcal{D}}[g(x) = 1].

Definition A.13 (Selective Risk). The selective risk is

R(f,g) = \frac{\mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}}[\ell(f(x), y) \cdot g(x)]}{\text{cov}(f,g)}

for loss \ell.

Theorem A.1 (Risk–Coverage Tradeoff). For any selective predictor, R(f,g) is non-decreasing in \text{cov}(f,g) under optimal selection. Conversely, higher coverage implies weakly higher risk.

Proof. Standard (El-Yaniv & Wiener 2010). \square

Remark A.3. H-SYNC's evaluation metrics are the institutional analogue of selective risk. Coverage corresponds to the fraction of claims assigned a non-U state; risk corresponds to FalseCertaintyRate; abstention corresponds to U.

A.6 Time and Asynchrony

Definition A.14 (Characteristic Time). Let f: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \to \mathbb{R} be a monotone response function. The characteristic time \tau is the time at which f reaches 1 - 1/e of its asymptotic value.

Definition A.15 (Logistic Response). The logistic function is

f(t) = \frac{L}{1 + e^{-k(t - t_0)}}

with asymptote L, rate k, midpoint t_0. The characteristic time is \tau = 1/k.

Proposition A.6 (Kim's Asynchrony Decomposition). Under coupled logistic models, the social burden integral admits a decomposition in which the timing parameter t_0 dominates the rate parameter k.

Proof. Skipped. Refer to Kim (2025). The decomposition is not re-derived here because the present authors have not verified the original computation.

Remark A.4. This appendix section is deliberately minimal. The synchronization gap \Gamma is a framing device, not a load-bearing component of the protocol. It does not appear in any state transition or metric.

