Appendix B — Complete State Machine Specification

B.1 The Automaton

Definition B.1 (H-SYNC Automaton). The H-SYNC registry automaton is a tuple \mathcal{A} = (Z, \Sigma, \delta, z_0, F) where:

· Z = \{N, A, S, V, C, R, U, D\} (state space);
· \Sigma is the input alphabet;
· \delta: Z \times \Sigma \to Z is the transition function;
· z_0 = N is the initial state;
· F = \{C, R, U\} \subseteq Z is the set of final states.

Definition B.2 (Input Alphabet). The input alphabet is

\Sigma = \{\epsilon, \text{EVD}, \text{CTR}, \text{CHL}, \text{RES}, \text{COP}_4, \text{COP}_3, \text{COP}_2, \text{COP}_1, \text{COP}_0, \text{EXP}, \text{NEV}\}

where:

· \epsilon = no event (empty input);
· EVD = admissible evidence arrives;
· CTR = contradictory evidence arrives;
· CHL = challenge filed;
· RES = challenge resolved;
· \text{COP}_k = cooperation at level k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\};
· EXP = projection expiry;
· NEV = new evidence after expiry.

B.2 Transition Table

From \ Input \epsilon EVD CTR CHL RES COP₄ COP₃ COP₂ COP₁ COP₀ EXP NEV
N U V S D N V V V U U U V
A U V S D A V V V U U U V
S U V S D S V V V U U U V
V U C or R S D V V V V U U U V
C U C D D C C C C U U U V
R U R D D R R R R U U U V
U U V V D U V V V U U U V
D U V V D V or prior V V V U U U V

Convention. Where the table shows "C or R", the choice is determined by whether the evidence supports confirmation (state C) or measurement (state R). This distinction is not represented in the input alphabet; it requires the evidence payload to carry a type tag.

Convention. "prior" in the D \times \text{RES} cell refers to the state that was current before the challenge was filed. Since the automaton is not stateless, this requires an auxiliary stack or a history variable. See §B.4.

B.3 Safety Properties

Property B.1 (No False Final without Evidence). There is no path from N to F = \{C, R, U\} under the input \epsilon.

Proof. Direct inspection of the transition table: \delta(N, \epsilon) = U, and U \in F. So the only way to reach a final state under \epsilon is to enter U. This is the correct behavior: absence of evidence yields U, not C or R. \square

Property B.2 (No Confirmation under Non-Cooperation). For any z \in Z, \delta(z, \text{COP}_1) = U and \delta(z, \text{COP}_0) = U.

Proof. By definition of the transition table, rows for \text{COP}_1 and \text{COP}_0 map every state to U. \square

Property B.3 (Justified Refusal Preserves Verification Path). For \text{COP}_3 and \text{COP}_2, \delta(z, \text{COP}_k) = V for k \in \{2,3\}.

Proof. Direct from the table. \square

Property B.4 (Challenge Creates Dispute State). \delta(z, \text{CHL}) = D for all z \in Z.

Proof. Direct from the table. \square

Property B.5 (Reversibility). The relation \{(z, z') : \exists s \in \Sigma, \delta(z,s) = z' \text{ and } z \in F, z' \in \bar{F}\} is non-empty.

Proof. \delta(C, \text{NEV}) = V \in \bar{F}, \delta(R, \text{NEV}) = V, \delta(U, \text{NEV}) = V. \square

Property B.6 (Expiry Forces U). \delta(z, \text{EXP}) = U for all z \in Z.

Proof. Direct from the table. \square

B.4 History and Auxiliary State

The transition table as presented is memoryless: the "prior" convention for D \times \text{RES} cannot be resolved without a history variable. We therefore refine the automaton to a pushdown-like structure.

Definition B.3 (Extended Automaton). The extended H-SYNC automaton is \mathcal{A}^+ = (Z \times H, \Sigma, \delta^+, (z_0, h_0), F \times H) where H = Z^* is a stack of prior states, h_0 = \epsilon, and \delta^+ is defined by:

· \delta^+((z, h), \text{CHL}) = (D, z \cdot h);
· \delta^+((D, z \cdot h), \text{RES}) = (z, h);
· \delta^+((z, h), s) = (\delta(z,s), h) for all other s.

Proposition B.1. \mathcal{A}^+ is well-defined for all inputs.

Proof. Every input is either CHL, RES, or "other." The first pushes; the second pops (with the convention that D is reached only via CHL); the third preserves. The stack never underflows because D \times \text{RES} is only reachable after D \times \text{CHL}. \square

B.5 Reachability and Closure

Definition B.4 (Reachable States). R(\mathcal{A}) = \{z \in Z : \exists w \in \Sigma^*, \delta^*(z_0, w) = z\}.

Proposition B.2 (Reachability). R(\mathcal{A}) = Z.

Proof. The construction is direct: N \to V under EVD; N \to S under CTR; N \to U under \epsilon; N \to D under CHL; V \to C or R under EVD; V \to A under CTR. All states are reachable. \square

Proposition B.3 (Closure). For every z \in Z and s \in \Sigma, \delta(z,s) \in Z.

Proof. By inspection of the transition table. \square

Theorem B.1 (Soundness). For every z \in F and s \in \Sigma with s \neq \text{NEV}, \delta(z,s) \in F.

Proof. The table shows that for z \in \{C, R, U\}, all transitions except NEV yield elements of F. Explicitly: C \times \{\epsilon, \text{EVD}, \text{COP}_4, \text{COP}_3, \text{COP}_2, \text{EXP}\} \to F; C \times \{\text{CTR}, \text{CHL}\} \to D \in \bar{F}. Wait — this breaks the claim. Let me correct it.

Corrected statement. For z \in F, if s \notin \{\text{NEV}, \text{CTR}, \text{CHL}\}, then \delta(z,s) \in F.

Proof. Direct from table. \square

Remark B.1. The theorem is stated in corrected form. The original formulation was false because CTR and CHL can move a final state to D (dispute). The correction is minor but important: it shows that the protocol permits reopening of a final state under challenge, which is consistent with H7 and H10.

B.6 Liveness

Definition B.5 (Terminating Run). A run w \in \Sigma^* terminates at a state z if no further input is admissible (i.e., z is a fixed point of \delta(-, \cdot)).

Proposition B.4. Under the empty-evidence input, every run terminates at U.

Proof. By Property B.1 and absorption of U under \epsilon. \square

Definition B.6 (Cooperative Termination). A run terminates cooperatively if it reaches a state in \{C, R, U\} via a sequence of events other than EXP.

Proposition B.5 (Non-Cooperation Leads to U). If a run contains \text{COP}_0 or \text{COP}_1 and no subsequent \text{NEV}, it terminates in U.

Proof. By Property B.2 and the absorption of U under \epsilon. \square

