Appendix C — Complete Proofs

C.1 Proposition 1 (Sign Interpretation)

Statement. \Gamma(t) > 0 \iff \tau_S(t) > \tau_A(t).

Proof. \Gamma(t) = \log(\tau_S(t)/\tau_A(t)). Since \log is strictly increasing on (0, \infty) and \log(1) = 0, we have \log(\tau_S/\tau_A) > 0 \iff \tau_S/\tau_A > 1 \iff \tau_S > \tau_A. \square

C.2 Proposition 2 (Provenance ≠ Validity)

Statement. \text{Prov}(c) \nRightarrow \text{Valid}(c).

Proof. By counterexample. Construct a claim c with a fully reconstructible provenance chain in which the asserted quantity is S_{\text{claimed}}(c) = 100. Suppose every source, method, and actor in the chain is authentic and the DAG is acyclic and publicly traversable. It does not follow that 100 is correct. The provenance chain documents how the claim was produced, not whether the method was adequate or the sources accurate. A provenance check verifies reconstructibility, not correctness. Therefore provenance does not imply validity. \square

C.3 Proposition 3 (Absorbing U under Non-Cooperation)

Statement. If non-cooperation (\text{COP}_0 or \text{COP}_1) persists indefinitely, the state converges to U.

Proof. By Property B.2, \delta(z, \text{COP}_1) = U and \delta(z, \text{COP}_0) = U for every z \in Z. Once in U, by (T1′), \delta(U, \epsilon) = U. If no further event occurs, the state is fixed at U. Therefore under persistent non-cooperation and absent new evidence, the state converges to U after at most one non-cooperation event. \square

C.4 Proposition 4 (Sound Abstention — Corrected)

Statement. If evidence is insufficient for a final claim, H-SYNC returns U rather than a state in \{C, R\}.

Proof. Let c be a claim with evidence state E_t = \emptyset (no admissible evidence). By Property B.1, the only transition from N under \epsilon is to U. By Property B.2 (extended to \epsilon via T2), any other state transitions to U under \emptyset evidence. Therefore the state becomes U, which is in F but not in \{C, R\}. \square

Remark C.1. The v0.2 formulation was: "the only non-final state is U." This was false; N, A, S, V, D are also non-final. The corrected statement restricts to final states, in which case U is the unique final state reachable under \emptyset evidence (since C and R require evidence).

C.5 Proposition 5 (Non-Monotonicity)

Statement. The map t \mapsto \text{State}(c, t) is not monotone in the resolution order \sqsubseteq.

Proof. Consider a claim c that reaches state C at time t_1. By H10 and the transition table, \delta(C, \text{NEV}) = V. Since V \sqsubseteq C, the state has moved down the resolution order. This contradicts monotonicity. \square

C.6 Proposition 6 (Traceability — Corrected)

Statement. If H1 and H6 hold, then every claim in the registry is either publicly verifiable, auditor-verifiable, or in state U.

Proof. Let c be a claim. By H1, c has a provenance chain P. By H6, P is versioned. By Definition 15, P is either publicly reconstructible, auditor-reconstructible, or neither. If publicly reconstructible, c is publicly verifiable. If auditor-reconstructible but not public, c is auditor-verifiable. If neither, then by the adoption hook (§6.6), c is assigned state U. \square

C.7 Proposition 7 (Non-Cooperation Implies Abstention)

Statement. If a producer refuses cooperation at level 0 or 1, or at level 2 or 3 with unjustified status, and no independent verifier is available, the claim state is U.

Proof. By H11 and Property B.2, \delta(z, \text{COP}_0) = \delta(z, \text{COP}_1) = U for all z \in Z. For level 2 or 3 with unjustified status, the justification flag is 0, and by Definition 19 the refusal is treated as unjustified. The transition is therefore to U by T5. \square

C.8 Proposition 8 (Justified Refusal Does Not Automatically Yield U)

Statement. A justified refusal at level 2 or 3 does not by itself produce U if an independent verifier with appropriate authorization can reconstruct the provenance chain.

Proof. By Property B.3, \delta(z, \text{COP}_2) = \delta(z, \text{COP}_3) = V. In state V, by (T6), new evidence can transition to C or R. By Definition 15, an auditor-verifiable claim can be reconstructed under confidentiality. Therefore the state need not be U. \square

C.9 Proposition 9 (Polarity Symmetry — Weakened)

Statement. H11 applies regardless of whether the producer's claim is inflated or deflated relative to ground truth.

Proof. H11 is a condition on access, not on the sign of the claim error. The transition table does not depend on the sign of S_{\text{claimed}} - S_{\text{realized}}. Therefore the rule is sign-symmetric. \square

Remark C.2. The incentive is not sign-symmetric. For an inflated claim, U is better for the producer than C adverse. For a deflated claim, U is also better than C adverse. In both cases, U is weakly preferred by a producer seeking to avoid adverse findings. Thus the protocol may face an abstention incentive: producers may prefer refusal to cooperation. This is an open problem. Potential mitigations: mandatory publication of the U-list, sunset clauses forcing re-verification, and stake-weighted penalties for persistent U-status.

C.10 Additional Lemmas and Theorems

Lemma C.1 (U Is Absorbing under \epsilon). \delta(U, \epsilon) = U.

Proof. By (T1′) extended: \delta(N, \emptyset) = U and once in U, no evidence arrives, so U is fixed. \square

Lemma C.2 (No Path from U to C under \epsilon). There is no run w \in \{\epsilon\}^* with \delta^*(U, w) = C.

Proof. By Lemma C.1, U is a fixed point under \epsilon. \square

Lemma C.3 (State Space Is Closed). \delta[Z \times \Sigma] \subseteq Z.

Proof. By Proposition B.3. \square

Theorem C.1 (Progress). For every non-final state z \in \bar{F}, there exists an input s \in \Sigma such that \delta(z, s) \in F \cup \bar{F} (i.e., every non-final state has a transition).

Proof. By inspection of the transition table. For each z \in \bar{F}, at least one input (in fact, all inputs) yields a defined output. \square

Theorem C.2 (Determinism). \delta is a function, not a relation.

Proof. The table assigns at most one output to each (state, input) pair. The one ambiguity — "C or R" — is resolved by the input payload's type tag, which is part of the input, not the state. \square

Theorem C.3 (Reversibility). For every z \in F, there exists s \in \Sigma and z' \in \bar{F} with \delta(z,s) = z'.

Proof. \delta(C, \text{NEV}) = V; \delta(R, \text{NEV}) = V; \delta(U, \text{NEV}) = V. \square

Definition C.1 (Trusted Reversal). A reversal is trusted if the new evidence is independently verified. Untrusted reversals may exist but are marked with a version flag.

Theorem C.4 (Bounded Revision). Every final state can be revised at most once per cycle, where a cycle is defined by the sequence (final, NEV, verification, final).

Proof. By the transition table, NEV moves to V. From V, only EVD or CTR can move to another final state or to S. Once back in F, another NEV is required for further revision. \square

