Appendix D — Architecture

D.1 Component Overview

The H-SYNC architecture consists of five components.

1. Registry — an append-only, hash-linked log of claims and events.
2. Schema — a JSON Schema specification of claim payloads.
3. Engine — the state transducer implementing \delta.
4. Interface — the protocol by which institutions submit claims and receive state.
5. Auditor Subsystem — verifier management, sortition, and COI tracking.

D.2 Registry

Definition D.1 (Registry). A registry is a sequence of blocks B_0, B_1, \ldots where each block B_i contains:

· B_i.\text{id}: a unique identifier;
· B_i.\text{parent}: B_{i-1}.\text{id};
· B_i.\text{hash}: H(B_i.\text{payload} \| B_{i-1}.\text{hash});
· B_i.\text{payload}: a set of events (claim submissions, evidence arrivals, challenges, cooperation events, state transitions);
· B_i.\text{timestamp}: a timestamp;
· B_i.\text{signature}: a signature by the registry operator.

Property D.1 (Tamper-Evidence). Any modification to a block invalidates all subsequent blocks.

Proof. If B_i is modified, its hash changes, invalidating B_{i+1}.\text{parent}. By induction, all later blocks are invalidated. \square

Property D.2 (Append-Only). Blocks are never deleted or reordered.

Design invariant.

Property D.3 (Open U-List). The set of claims in state U is published at every block.

Design invariant. This is the minimum viable adoption hook.

