D.4 Engine

The engine is a state transducer with the following signature:

```
engine(claim_id: UUID, events: List[Event]) -> State
```

Algorithm.

```
1. z ← N
2. for each event e in events (ordered by timestamp):
3.     z ← δ(z, e.kind, e.payload)
4. return z
```

Complexity. O(n) in the number of events, assuming constant-time table lookup. With provenance traversal, the complexity becomes O(n + |V_P| + |E_P|).

Determinism. By Theorem C.2, \delta is deterministic. Therefore engine is deterministic given the same event sequence.

D.5 Interface Protocol

The interface is a REST API with the following endpoints.

Endpoint Method Description
/claim POST Submit a claim
/claim/{id} GET Retrieve current state
/claim/{id}/events GET Retrieve event history
/challenge POST File a challenge
/cooperation POST Record a cooperation event
/verify POST Submit verification evidence
/u-list GET Retrieve the current U-list
/audit GET Retrieve the audit trail

Protocol invariants.

· Every request is signed by the producer or verifier.
· Every response includes the current block hash.
· No endpoint permits state mutation outside the transition table.
· The u-list endpoint is unauthenticated and rate-limited.

D.6 Auditor Subsystem

Definition D.2 (Verifier Pool). A verifier pool P is a finite set of verifier identities v_1, \ldots, v_k, each with:

· an affiliation;
· a funding disclosure;
· a prior-engagement history;
· a version tag V_P.

Definition D.3 (Sortition). For a claim c requiring verification, verifiers are selected by uniform random draw from P \setminus \text{COI}(c), where \text{COI}(c) is the set of verifiers with a declared conflict of interest in c.

Property D.4 (Independence Bound — Informal). Under sortition from a pool P in which at most fraction \phi of verifiers are captured, the probability that a given claim is verified only by captured verifiers is at most \phi^k for a k-verifier assignment.

Proof. Under uniform random draw without replacement, the probability that all k verifiers are captured is \binom{\phi |P|}{k} / \binom{|P|}{k} \leq \phi^k. \square

Remark D.1. This bound is vacuous if \phi = 1 (fully captured pool). The bound is therefore a conditional guarantee, not an unconditional one. This is the formal statement of the v0.2 criticism that Prop. 6 was circular. We state it here as an explicit condition.

D.7 Cooperation Subsystem

The cooperation subsystem maintains a graded ledger.

Definition D.4 (Cooperation Record). A cooperation record is a tuple (c, L, J, t, \text{basis}) where c is the claim, L \in \{0,1,2,3,4\} is the level, J \in \{0,1\} is the justification flag, t is the timestamp, and basis is the cited legal authority (if any).

Definition D.5 (Justification Function). The justification function J: \text{LegalBasis} \to \{0,1\} maps a cited basis to a binary flag:

· Privacy statutes: J = 1;
· Litigation holds: J = 1;
· Statutory confidentiality: J = 1;
· No basis cited: J = 0;
· Basis not recognized: J = 0.

Aggregation Rule. For a claim with multiple cooperation events, the effective level is the minimum level and the effective justification is the AND of all flags.

