Appendix E — Metrics: Formal Development

E.1 Selective Risk Formalization

Let \mathcal{C} be a finite set of claims, and let \text{state}: \mathcal{C} \to Z be the engine's output. Let \text{truth}: \mathcal{C} \to \{\text{correct}, \text{incorrect}\} be a ground-truth labeling.

Definition E.1 (Coverage).

\text{Coverage} = \frac{|\{c : \text{state}(c) \neq U\}|}{|\mathcal{C}|}.

Definition E.2 (VerifiedResolution).

\text{VR} = \frac{|\{c : \text{state}(c) \in \{C, R\} \wedge \text{truth}(c) = \text{correct}\}|}{|\mathcal{C}|}.

Definition E.3 (JustifiedAbstention).

\text{JA} = \frac{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = U \wedge \text{truth}(c) = \text{correct}\}|}{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = U\}|}.

Definition E.4 (FalseCertaintyRate).

\text{FCR} = \frac{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = C \wedge \text{truth}(c) = \text{incorrect}\}|}{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = C\}|}.

Convention. If |\{c : \text{state}(c) = C\}| = 0, set \text{FCR} = 0 and exclude the run from comparative FCR analysis.

Definition E.5 (Unjustified Attribution Rate).

\text{UAR} = \frac{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = C \wedge \text{attribution}(c) = \text{contestable}\}|}{|\{c : \text{state}(c) = C \wedge \text{attribution}(c) \neq \bot\}|}.

E.2 Stake-Weighted Metrics

Let \sigma: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}_{>0} be a stake function (e.g., dollar value). Replace cardinalities with sums of \sigma.

Definition E.6 (Stake-Weighted FCR).

\text{FCR}_\sigma = \frac{\sum_{c \in \text{bad}_C} \sigma(c)}{\sum_{c \in C\text{-state}} \sigma(c)}.

E.3 Pareto Frontier

The evaluation is a triple (\text{VR}, \text{JA}, \text{FCR}). A protocol is Pareto-efficient if no other protocol improves one component without degrading another.

Proposition E.1. The triple (\text{VR}, \text{JA}, \text{FCR}) admits incomparable optima.

Proof. By construction, a protocol that always outputs U has \text{JA} = 1, \text{FCR} = 0, \text{VR} = 0. A protocol that always outputs C has \text{VR} > 0, \text{FCR} \to 1, \text{JA} undefined. Neither dominates the other. \square

Remark E.1. The scalarization \text{VR} \times (1 - \text{FCR}) is a common but lossy summary. We discourage it.

E.4 Statistical Properties

Definition E.7 (Calibration). A protocol is calibrated if, over claims assigned state C, the empirical frequency of correctness equals the claimed confidence (where confidence is provided in the uncertainty field).

Proposition E.2. H-SYNC does not by itself guarantee calibration.

Proof. Calibration is a property of the underlying verification process, not of the state machine. \square

